WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:02.580
We all know about Nazis.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:06.299
Nazis were violent and powerful Nazi regime
led by Adolf Hitler.

00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:11.400
Though their crimes against humanity can never
be forgiven, Nazis space/missile program was

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.920
no doubt one of the most advanced programs
of it’s time.

00:00:14.920 --> 00:00:18.360
And, without it, the American’s may have
never reached the moon.

00:00:18.360 --> 00:00:24.730
So, here’s how Hitler helped America win
the space race.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:29.090
One of the largest assets America received
from Nazi Germany was not technology, but

00:00:29.090 --> 00:00:32.800
actually a world renowned rocket scientist:
Wernher Von Braun.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:38.190
Braun was originally born in Wirsitz Germany
in 1912 right before the first World War.

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:42.900
Luckily, Braun was born to a rather prosperous
family which allowed him to pursue his passions

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.120
and interests from a very young age.

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:48.989
Given that he would eventually become a rocket
scientist, it’s not surprising to hear that

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:52.760
he was extremely interested in astronomy and
space from a very young age.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:56.989
Apparently, he received a telescope as a gift
from his mom, and he would use it to observe

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:58.829
the stars all the time.

00:00:58.829 --> 00:01:03.440
Despite his interest in astronomy and space
though, he wasn’t particularly good at math

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.650
or physics.

00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.649
In fact, he didn’t do very well in school
up until age 13 when he read the book “The

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:12.609
Rocket into Interplanetary Space” by Hermann
Oberth.

00:01:12.609 --> 00:01:16.709
He didn’t suddenly become smart after reading
this book or anything like that.

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:20.810
School was still very much a massive challenge
for him, but the book motivated him to work

00:01:20.810 --> 00:01:22.170
much harder.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:26.759
And this work would eventually land him a
seat at the Berlin Institute of Technology.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:30.429
During his time here, Braun would attempt
to get as involved as possible with rocket

00:01:30.429 --> 00:01:31.590
development.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.840
Two notable examples of this were his membership
in the German Society for Space Travel and

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.350
his tendency to help Oberth with liquid fuel
rocket motor tests.

00:01:39.350 --> 00:01:43.819
If you guys don’t remember, Oberth was the
guy who wrote the Rocket into Interplanetary

00:01:43.819 --> 00:01:48.490
Space, so Braun basically had the opportunity
to work with his childhood hero.

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:52.399
Braun didn’t take this opportunity lightly,
and worked hard to impress both Oberth and

00:01:52.399 --> 00:01:55.829
the captain of the German Society for Space
travel Walter Dornberger.

00:01:55.829 --> 00:02:00.859
Eventually, Braun would graduate from the
Berlin Institute of Technology in 1932, and

00:02:00.859 --> 00:02:04.719
he would enroll in the University of Berlin
to complete his PhD in physics.

00:02:04.719 --> 00:02:08.879
Unlike most research papers, which Elon Musk
suggests are useless, Braun’s research was

00:02:08.879 --> 00:02:11.209
definitely not useless.

00:02:11.209 --> 00:02:15.760
His doctoral thesis covered his research and
development of a 300 and 660 pound thrust

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.769
rocket engine.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:21.579
In fact, his research was considered a military
security risk and so his research paper was

00:02:21.579 --> 00:02:23.989
simply titled “About Combustion Tests”.

00:02:23.989 --> 00:02:27.720
Braun would quickly turn around and implement
his research with a couple of engineers.

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:32.520
Together, they would end up launching two
rockets by December of 1934 both of which

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:35.790
were successful and reached an altitude of
2.4 km.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:39.360
Keep in mind that Braun was only 22 years
old at this point.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:43.430
Despite all of this progress though, Braun’s
future with rockets was cloudy.

00:02:43.430 --> 00:02:48.519
The German Society for Space Travel was facing
massive financial issues, and Captain Dornberger

00:02:48.519 --> 00:02:51.920
actually had to get a special research grant
to fund Braun’s two rockets.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:56.310
Unfortunately, the society would end up going
under, and this effectively cut off rocket

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:57.950
funding for Braun.

00:02:57.950 --> 00:03:02.379
This was especially problematic as private
rocket tests were forbidden, so Braun had

00:03:02.379 --> 00:03:04.510
to get funding through the military.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:08.969
Fortunately for Braun, a new chancellor had
just come into power and this man had extremely

00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:10.860
grand visions for Germany.

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.560
Hitler wanted to show the world how technologically
advanced Germany was.

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:18.799
He not only wanted to develop rockets/missiles
that were more powerful than any other country,

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:22.020
but he wanted Germany to be the first to reach
the moon.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:26.239
Hitler knew he had to go step by step though,
so he funded rocket development as soon as

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:27.310
possible.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:32.189
Hitler would scrap the rocket testing grounds
in Berlin which he deemed were way too small.

00:03:32.189 --> 00:03:36.349
Instead he set up a massive military development
facility in Peenemunde.

00:03:36.349 --> 00:03:41.690
Hitler would appoint Dornberger as the military
commander and Braun as the technical director.

00:03:41.690 --> 00:03:46.329
And with that, Hitler ordered the team to
get to work on the famous German V2 rocket.

00:03:46.329 --> 00:03:50.510
Now, Braun really had no ambitions to develop
weapons of mass destruction.

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:55.270
He really just wanted to develop rockets for
space research and eventually civilian travel.

00:03:55.270 --> 00:03:58.830
Given that this was his only opportunity to
work on rockets though, he would take the

00:03:58.830 --> 00:03:59.859
job.

00:03:59.859 --> 00:04:03.590
Over the next several years, Braun would slowly
iterate through rocket designs beginning with

00:04:03.590 --> 00:04:05.300
the A1 rocket that he previously launched
during his PhD research.

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:11.870
After a couple of iterations, in 1942, Braun
would land on the A4 rocket aka the V2 rocket.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:16.550
This rocket was able to hit locations that
were 315 km away, something that no other

00:04:16.550 --> 00:04:18.900
country could do at the time.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:23.540
As we discussed though, this was only the
beginning and Hitler had much bigger visions.

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:29.560
So, just as the A4 rocket was perfected, Hitler
ordered Braun to develop the A-10 rocket.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:34.370
Hitler wanted the A-10 rocket to boast a 100
tonne thrust engine capable of hitting targets

00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:35.990
900 km in distance.

00:04:35.990 --> 00:04:40.360
He was hoping to eventually modify this rocket
to support crews and send Nazis to space and

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:41.360
the moon.

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:45.660
Knowing this, Braun not only worked on the
regular A-10 rocket but also the modified

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.710
version.The modified version was a combination
of the A-10 rocket and the Me 262 German fighter

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:51.710
jet.

00:04:51.710 --> 00:04:55.950
Braun knew that spaceflight required strong
fuel efficiency and good aerodynamics as opposed

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:57.130
to just raw power.

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.180
So, that was the reasoning behind combining
the rocket with a fighter jet.

00:05:01.180 --> 00:05:04.280
Hitler would never see this project through
though as he is thought to have committed

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:07.020
suicide at the end of World War 2.

00:05:07.020 --> 00:05:11.320
Surprisingly though, Braun would end up seeing
this project through till the very end.

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:16.620
You see, after D day in 1944, Braun realized
that Hitler had little chance at winning the

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:17.620
war.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:22.190
Braun would flee the V2 rocket complex before
the allies captured, but he and his team would

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:25.100
end up surrendering to the Americans in Bavaria.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:28.520
Braun would explain to the US authorities
that he never had any intention to actually

00:05:28.520 --> 00:05:32.070
harm civilians and that he was simply a rocket
scientist.

00:05:32.070 --> 00:05:35.780
Whether the authorities believed him is something
that we’ll never know.

00:05:35.780 --> 00:05:39.590
What they knew for sure though was that Braun
was a brilliant rocket scientist.

00:05:39.590 --> 00:05:43.490
After all, the man was literally working on
sending the Nazi leader to the moon at age

00:05:43.490 --> 00:05:44.490
32.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:48.560
So, they decided that whether he was telling
the truth or not, his knowledge would be extremely

00:05:48.560 --> 00:05:50.430
valuable to the Americans.

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:55.140
Braun and 1600 of his Nazi scientist peers
would be assigned to a top secret US intelligence

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:58.170
program called operation paperclip.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:06:02.270
Operation paperclip was established by president
Harry Truman and was designed to harness as

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:05.930
much information as possible from top Nazi
scientists.

00:06:05.930 --> 00:06:08.640
President Truman ordered that no Nazi supporters
should be recruited.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.000
However, there are several reports suggesting
that the OSS which would later become the

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.860
CIA went ahead and eliminated all incriminating
evidence linking these scientists to war crimes.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:23.540
Though that is no doubt an ethically questionable
move, the operation is thought to be essential.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:28.140
The Soviet Union was doing the exact with
a set of Nazi scientists that they had captured.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:33.380
So, the US would be at a disadvantage to the
USSR if they didn’t go ahead and take advantage

00:06:33.380 --> 00:06:35.620
of Nazi scientists as well.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:39.970
We don’t really know what all the US learned
from these scientists, but it of course includes

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.460
significant knowledge about German missiles
and rockets.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:49.250
Operation paperclip would take place between
1945 and 1959 at which point, the US was satisfied

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.610
that they had gathered more than enough information
from the scientists.

00:06:52.610 --> 00:06:56.370
As a result, the scientists would be released
and allowed to just become regular American

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:57.410
citizens.

00:06:57.410 --> 00:07:01.270
As for Braun specifically, he would become
the technical director of the US Army ballistic

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:06.570
weapon program in 1952, and he would become
a US citizen in 1955.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:11.680
This is quite ironic as just a decade before
that, he was the leader of the Nazi rocket

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:12.680
program.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:17.740
Anyway, right as operation paperclip was closed
up, President Eisenhower would establish NASA.

00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:22.460
Braun and his entire team would be transferred
from the Army to NASA and Braun would be made

00:07:22.460 --> 00:07:25.220
the director of NASA’s marshall space flight
center.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:30.500
This sounds like it's straight out of a movie
or a book, but somehow this actually happened.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:34.840
Within two decades, Von Braun went from trying
to get Nazis to the moon to trying to get

00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:36.430
America to the moon.

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:40.020
Aside from being the director of the flight
center, Braun was the chief architect of the

00:07:40.020 --> 00:07:41.250
Saturn rockets.

00:07:41.250 --> 00:07:47.430
He led the development of the Saturn I, IB,
and V. And as we all know, the Saturn V would

00:07:47.430 --> 00:07:51.620
end up being the rocket used in Apollo 11
to get Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin to the

00:07:51.620 --> 00:07:52.620
moon.

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:57.770
Somehow, Braun’s childhood dream was fulfilled,
probably in a way that he never anticipated.

00:07:57.770 --> 00:08:01.210
What’s just as surprising though is the
Saturn V rocket.

00:08:01.210 --> 00:08:06.220
To this day, the Saturn V is still the tallest
and the most powerful rocket ever.

00:08:06.220 --> 00:08:11.280
Starship is looking to dethrone the Saturn
V, but at this point it doesn’t even matter.

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:16.430
I mean the Saturn V has held that title for
over 50 years which just goes to show how

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:18.920
far ahead Braun and his team were.

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:22.800
After the Apollo 11 mission, Braun would be
promoted to deputy associate administrator

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:24.970
of Nasa in 1970.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.610
However, he didn’t stay around for long
there.

00:08:27.610 --> 00:08:32.460
He had already accomplished his dream of sending
humans to the moon and NASA was starting to

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:33.669
receive crippling budget cuts.

00:08:33.669 --> 00:08:38.390
So, Braun would resign from his position at
NASA in 1972 and he would become the vice

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:40.430
president of Fairchild industries.

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:44.730
Unfortunately, that same year, Braun would
be diagnosed with kidney cancer.

00:08:44.730 --> 00:08:48.720
Despite the diagnosis, Braun would continue
working at Fairchild industries, and he would

00:08:48.720 --> 00:08:52.170
start up one more project before his death
in 1977.

00:08:52.170 --> 00:08:56.840
In 1975, he founded the national space institute
which would eventually become the national

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.220
space society in 1987.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:04.120
Somehow poetically, Braun’s rocket journey
began with a space society and it ended with

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:05.650
a space society.

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:09.910
Near the end of his life, Braun would win
two major awards which were the NASA distinguished

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:15.220
service medal in 1969 and the national medal
of science in engineering in 1977.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:19.780
The first award is the highest award given
out by NASA and the second award is the highest

00:09:19.780 --> 00:09:22.150
science award given out by the US.

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:27.540
Today, Braun’s legacy can be found in history
book’s and museums, but the average person

00:09:27.540 --> 00:09:31.600
really isn’t aware of this extremely accomplished
man likely because of his checkered past with

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:32.680
the Nazis.

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:36.420
People familiar with his work, however, are
extremely complementary of Braun.

00:09:36.420 --> 00:09:40.670
Apollo program director Sam Philps for instance
originally said that he did not think that

00:09:40.670 --> 00:09:44.940
the US would have reached the moon as quickly
if it wasn’t for Von Braun.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:48.920
After discussing with his colleagues, however,
he revised this statement asserting that he

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.830
did not believe that the US would have reached
the moon at all if it wasn’t for Von Braun.

00:09:53.830 --> 00:09:56.340
Did you guys know about this German Rocket
prodigy?

